VALUES ('a1ad165a-7df4-48be-9efc-9f341ccb5117', 'aws-saa-practice-5', 'AWS SAA-C03 Practice Paper 5', 'aws-saa', ARRAY['WAF Rate Limiting', 'Permissions Boundary', 'PrivateLink', 'Object Lock', 'Shield Advanced', 'Macie', 'SQS DLQ', 'Transit Gateway', 'Aurora Serverless']::TEXT[], 20, 'standard', true, 5, 30);

-- ── Domain: Design Secure Architectures (30% — 6 questions) ──

VALUES ('46c4ee26-e59e-430d-8361-a596e940e481', 'a1ad165a-7df4-48be-9efc-9f341ccb5117', 1, 'A public API endpoint behind an Application Load Balancer is being hit with an abnormally high rate of requests from a small set of IP addresses, consistent with a credential-stuffing or brute-force attempt. What AWS WAF feature specifically throttles requests from a given source once it exceeds a defined threshold?');
VALUES ('46c4ee26-e59e-430d-8361-a596e940e481', '9d40d600-94bb-42ae-a473-343eacb5a0d3', 'A WAF rate-based rule is specifically designed for exactly this pattern: it continuously tracks the number of requests received from each individual IP address (or another configurable aggregation key) over a rolling 5-minute window, and automatically triggers a configured action (commonly Block) once a specific IP crosses the defined threshold — directly mitigating credential-stuffing, brute-force login attempts, and application-layer (L7) DDoS-style abuse from a concentrated set of sources, without needing to block those IPs manually or permanently. A standard IP-match rule (allow/block a specific, manually-listed set of IPs) has no automatic RATE-tracking behavior of its own — it is a static list, not a dynamic threshold-based mechanism, a genuine distinction from a rate-based rule. KMS manages encryption keys (covered in Paper 1), entirely unrelated to request-rate throttling. WAF rate-based rules operate per-source specifically (not just an undifferentiated account-wide total), which is exactly what makes them effective against a small set of abusive sources without collaterally affecting all other legitimate traffic.');

VALUES ('85dc2347-a4a5-449c-9dc4-5d0331f4f714', 'a1ad165a-7df4-48be-9efc-9f341ccb5117', 2, 'A company delegates IAM permission-granting authority to team leads so they can create roles for their own team''s projects, but wants to guarantee that no team lead can ever grant a role permissions broader than a defined maximum, regardless of what the team lead''s own IAM policy allows them to attach. What IAM feature enforces this ceiling?');
VALUES ('85dc2347-a4a5-449c-9dc4-5d0331f4f714', 'e80513f8-96a7-41d0-be7c-36750f521781', 'A permissions boundary is an advanced IAM feature specifically designed for this kind of DELEGATED administration scenario: it is attached to a user or role and defines the maximum permissions that identity can ever have, regardless of what permissions policies are directly attached to it — the actual EFFECTIVE permissions are always the intersection (the overlap) of the attached policy and the permissions boundary, meaning even if a team lead attaches an overly broad policy (by mistake or otherwise) to a role they create, anything outside the permissions boundary is still denied. This is exactly what lets an organization safely delegate "create roles for your own team" without also delegating "grant unlimited privilege escalation" — a real, important distinction from SCPs (covered in Paper 3), which apply at the Organization/OU level to ALL accounts/principals beneath them, while permissions boundaries apply to a SPECIFIC individual user/role, a finer-grained tool for exactly this kind of delegated-administration guardrail. Resource-based policies (covered in earlier papers, e.g., S3 bucket policies) attach to a RESOURCE, not to the identity being capped — a different mechanism entirely.');

VALUES ('c15a8b55-cef0-42a4-96f9-ffc5bdd80f8a', 'a1ad165a-7df4-48be-9efc-9f341ccb5117', 3, 'An application in a private subnet needs to call a third-party SaaS API that is exposed via AWS PrivateLink (as an Interface VPC Endpoint), keeping the traffic entirely on the AWS network without traversing the public internet. How does an Interface Endpoint differ mechanically from the Gateway Endpoint used for S3/DynamoDB?');
VALUES ('c15a8b55-cef0-42a4-96f9-ffc5bdd80f8a', 'cf5582f5-8806-44e8-aac1-541c7e5c41c0', 'An Interface Endpoint (built on AWS PrivateLink) provisions an actual elastic network interface with a private IP address directly within the customer''s VPC subnet — traffic to the target service is sent to that private IP, keeping it on the AWS network without traversing the public internet, and this mechanism supports a much broader range of targets than just AWS services, including specifically third-party SaaS offerings that expose themselves via PrivateLink (exactly this scenario) as well as most AWS services generally. A Gateway Endpoint (covered in Paper 1), by contrast, works by adding a target in the VPC''s ROUTE TABLE (no ENI/IP address involved) and is specifically limited to only two targets: S3 and DynamoDB — this is the actual, testable mechanical and scope distinction between the two endpoint types, reversed from how option C incorrectly states it. Interface Endpoints do carry an hourly charge plus data processing charges (unlike Gateway Endpoints, which are free) — a real cost trade-off for the broader capability. VPC endpoints (Interface type specifically) are exactly the mechanism that DOES support reaching PrivateLink-enabled third-party SaaS services privately — this is a genuine, real, and increasingly common integration pattern.');

VALUES ('760df109-289c-4139-b3ad-a3755df2dc21', 'a1ad165a-7df4-48be-9efc-9f341ccb5117', 4, 'A financial services company must store certain records in S3 such that they CANNOT be deleted or overwritten by ANYONE — including the account root user — for a legally mandated retention period, to satisfy WORM (Write Once, Read Many) compliance requirements. What S3 feature provides this?');
VALUES ('760df109-289c-4139-b3ad-a3755df2dc21', 'efc011a1-eaf8-4f06-9d73-fbe655191186', 'S3 Object Lock provides genuine WORM protection, and Compliance mode specifically is the stricter of its two modes: once an object version is locked in Compliance mode for a retention period, NO user — including the AWS account''s own root user — can delete that version or shorten/remove its retention setting until the period expires; this is precisely the strong guarantee regulated industries (finance, healthcare, legal) often require to satisfy retention regulations. (Governance mode, the other Object Lock mode, is similar but allows users with a specific special IAM permission to override the protection — a real, testable distinction between the two modes, with Compliance mode being the one matching an absolute "cannot be deleted by anyone" requirement.) Object Lock requires S3 Versioning to be enabled as a prerequisite, but Versioning ALONE does not prevent deletion at all — a delete request on a versioned object without Object Lock simply adds a delete marker (recoverable, but not actually prevented), a real and important distinction from Object Lock''s actual prevention of the delete/overwrite action itself. Transfer Acceleration is an unrelated upload-speed feature (covered in earlier papers).');

VALUES ('6ecb9d3e-72ae-4b77-8e8d-e0a2d400d6f2', 'a1ad165a-7df4-48be-9efc-9f341ccb5117', 5, 'A company running a large e-commerce platform on AWS wants proactive DDoS protection with direct access to the AWS DDoS Response Team (DRT) during an active attack, cost protection against scaling charges incurred specifically due to a DDoS attack, and near-real-time visibility into attack details — beyond what the automatically-included baseline protection provides. What should they use?');
VALUES ('6ecb9d3e-72ae-4b77-8e8d-e0a2d400d6f2', 'ba21b73e-5c0d-45a4-9252-688b27006989', 'Shield Advanced is specifically the paid, opt-in tier that adds meaningfully more than Shield Standard''s automatic, free baseline (covered in Paper 2): direct access to the AWS DDoS Response Team (DRT) for hands-on assistance during an active, significant attack; cost protection reimbursing scaling charges (e.g., extra ALB/CloudFront/EC2 usage) that were specifically driven by a DDoS attack rather than genuine legitimate traffic growth; and detailed, near-real-time attack diagnostics and historical reporting — none of which Shield Standard provides, since Standard is intentionally a baseline, automatic, no-configuration-needed protection layer rather than the fuller, actively-supported service Advanced represents. WAF (covered in Paper 2) is a separate, complementary Layer-7 content-filtering service (and is actually included as part of Shield Advanced''s value, at no extra WAF charge) — but WAF itself has no DRT-access or cost-protection function of its own. AWS genuinely does provide this kind of premium, human-assisted DDoS response capability specifically through Shield Advanced.');

VALUES ('47594ee9-2847-4c99-8d9d-c311671a4e45', 'a1ad165a-7df4-48be-9efc-9f341ccb5117', 6, 'A company suspects that some of their S3 buckets, accumulated over years of ad hoc use, may contain personally identifiable information (PII) or other sensitive data that was never properly classified or secured, but manually reviewing millions of objects is impractical. What AWS service automatically discovers and classifies this kind of sensitive data within S3?');
VALUES ('47594ee9-2847-4c99-8d9d-c311671a4e45', '4e5900e3-a3e9-410d-a4a7-35e833b46c71', 'Macie is purpose-built specifically for automated sensitive-data discovery and classification within S3 — it uses machine learning and pattern matching to identify data types like PII (names, addresses, national ID numbers), financial data (credit card numbers), and credentials embedded in stored objects, then surfaces findings (which buckets/objects contain what kind of sensitive data, and how it''s currently secured/exposed) at a scale no manual review process could realistically achieve across millions of objects. AWS Config (covered in Paper 3) tracks resource CONFIGURATION state/compliance — it has no content-inspection capability of an object''s actual data. GuardDuty (covered in Paper 4) is a behavioral threat-detection service (network/API activity analysis) — also fundamentally different from inspecting object CONTENT for sensitive data patterns, a common category-confusion distractor between these security services that each serve genuinely different, non-overlapping purposes.');

-- ── Domain: Design Resilient Architectures (26% — 5 questions) ──

VALUES ('71495380-6956-4feb-9622-7d9fd90b6843', 'a1ad165a-7df4-48be-9efc-9f341ccb5117', 7, 'A company wants Route 53 to return SEVERAL healthy IP addresses in response to a single DNS query (not just one), allowing basic client-side load distribution and automatic exclusion of any address that fails its health check, without the more complex weighting of latency-based or weighted routing. What routing policy fits this?');
VALUES ('71495380-6956-4feb-9622-7d9fd90b6843', '6a2125ca-c52e-45b5-a492-6508f478f576', 'Multi-Value Answer routing is specifically designed for this "return several healthy values, simple client-side distribution" need: Route 53 returns up to 8 different record values selected (pseudo-)randomly per query, and — critically — can be configured with health checks so that a record currently failing its check is automatically excluded from what gets returned, giving basic resilience against a specific target being down without requiring the more sophisticated configuration of weighted routing (explicit traffic percentages) or latency-based routing (covered in Paper 4, routing to the lowest-latency Region). It is NOT a substitute for a real load balancer (it provides DNS-level, not connection-level, distribution — clients still pick from among the returned values themselves), but it is a real, lightweight, commonly used pattern for exactly this basic multi-target availability need. Simple routing (covered in Paper 4) returns a FIXED set of values with NO health-check awareness at all — the opposite of what''s being asked for here. Geolocation routing routes based on the requester''s location, an unrelated dimension from returning multiple health-checked values.');

VALUES ('658948b1-d935-4976-9201-02f723dbb530', 'a1ad165a-7df4-48be-9efc-9f341ccb5117', 8, 'A consumer application processing messages from an SQS queue occasionally fails to process a specific message due to a bug, and that message is redelivered and re-fails repeatedly, consuming processing capacity without ever succeeding, while also potentially blocking visibility into other, healthy messages. What SQS feature isolates these repeatedly-failing messages?');
VALUES ('658948b1-d935-4976-9201-02f723dbb530', '1bd3b9c4-ca65-4dd6-8b49-a59ddcfb8778', 'A Dead Letter Queue is a separate SQS queue configured as the redrive target for a source queue, along with a maxReceiveCount setting — once a specific message has been received/attempted the configured maximum number of times without being successfully processed and deleted, SQS automatically routes it to the DLQ instead of continuing to redeliver it to consumers, directly preventing a single problematic message from endlessly consuming processing attempts or obscuring visibility into the queue''s otherwise-healthy backlog. Messages in the DLQ can then be inspected separately (often revealing a genuine application bug, a malformed message, or a downstream dependency issue) without blocking normal queue processing. This is a real, standard, and frequently used SQS reliability pattern — SQS absolutely does provide this mechanism, rather than leaving messages to redeliver forever with no alternative. DynamoDB Global Tables (covered in Paper 3) and S3 Object Lock (covered earlier in this paper) both serve entirely unrelated functions with no connection to SQS message-failure handling.');

VALUES ('351d71bd-e172-4e39-9aae-f6e5e071a05f', 'a1ad165a-7df4-48be-9efc-9f341ccb5117', 9, 'A company deploying a web application wants AWS to automatically handle capacity provisioning, load balancing, auto scaling, and health monitoring for their application, while the company retains full control over the underlying application code, without manually configuring each of these pieces of infrastructure separately from scratch. What AWS service provides this?');
VALUES ('351d71bd-e172-4e39-9aae-f6e5e071a05f', 'c1d78989-52e6-4552-81bd-599b3d4b4cd0', 'Elastic Beanstalk is AWS''s managed platform-as-a-service offering specifically for this scenario: a developer uploads their application code (in a supported language/platform), and Beanstalk automatically provisions and manages the underlying infrastructure needed to run it — EC2 instances, an Elastic Load Balancer, an Auto Scaling Group, and health monitoring/auto-recovery — without the developer needing to configure each of those pieces individually from scratch the way they would building the same architecture manually (e.g., manually wiring together an ALB, ASG, and EC2 launch template). Critically, Beanstalk does not hide or lock away the underlying resources — a developer retains the ability to access and directly customize the EC2 instances, load balancer settings, or other resources if their needs go beyond Beanstalk''s defaults, distinguishing it from a fully black-box PaaS. Direct Connect (dedicated network connectivity) and Macie (sensitive-data discovery) both serve entirely unrelated functions from application infrastructure provisioning.');

VALUES ('1e9da3c0-85c2-498a-8ec6-9ea6c0a6c5ff', 'a1ad165a-7df4-48be-9efc-9f341ccb5117', 10, 'A company replicates critical data from their primary Region to a secondary Region using S3 Cross-Region Replication specifically to meet a disaster recovery requirement. What CRR characteristic is important to understand when calculating the achievable RPO (Recovery Point Objective) for this DR strategy?');
VALUES ('1e9da3c0-85c2-498a-8ec6-9ea6c0a6c5ff', 'b42aadbb-2f7b-4dc5-9864-48d26ae8b52e', 'CRR (covered in Paper 1) replicates asynchronously — the source write completes and is acknowledged before replication to the destination Region necessarily finishes, meaning there is normally SOME delay (typically measured in seconds under normal conditions) before a given write is actually present and durable in the destination bucket. This directly matters for RPO (introduced via BIA concepts in Security+ content, and directly relevant here in an AWS DR context): RPO represents the maximum acceptable data loss measured in time, and the realistic achievable RPO for a CRR-based DR strategy is bounded by this replication delay — if replication is still catching up at the exact moment of a primary-Region disaster, whatever hadn''t yet replicated is the data actually at risk of loss. For requirements needing a more predictable, tightly-bounded window, S3 Replication Time Control (RTC) is an available CRR enhancement that provides a defined replication time objective (commonly 15 minutes for 99.99% of objects) along with monitoring — a real, purpose-built answer for tighter RPO needs, rather than CRR''s baseline best-effort asynchronous behavior. CRR is not synchronous, does not guarantee zero delay, and replicates continuously (as writes occur), not on a monthly schedule.');

VALUES ('729fb6a1-e505-4a77-9cea-78409818fe79', 'a1ad165a-7df4-48be-9efc-9f341ccb5117', 11, 'An Application Load Balancer''s idle timeout is reached while a slow backend request is still processing, causing the ALB to close the client connection prematurely before the backend responds. What ALB setting should be reviewed and adjusted to resolve this, and what should guide the new value?');
VALUES ('729fb6a1-e505-4a77-9cea-78409818fe79', '4c244799-6c2f-4c1a-9c0f-ffb16923c2eb', 'The ALB idle timeout setting controls how long the load balancer will keep a connection open while waiting for the backend to send data — if a legitimate, slow-but-normal backend request takes longer than this configured timeout to respond, the ALB closes the connection prematurely, exactly the symptom described, and this is a genuinely common, real-world misconfiguration (the default is 60 seconds, which is too short for some legitimately slow backend operations like a large report generation or a complex query). The fix is to INCREASE the idle timeout to comfortably exceed the backend''s actual expected response time for its slowest legitimate requests — decreasing it (option C) would make the problem strictly worse, closing connections even sooner. This IS a configurable ALB setting, not fixed, and while NLBs have their own separate idle timeout characteristics, ALBs absolutely have and enforce this setting as well — it is directly relevant to this ALB-specific scenario.');

-- ── Domain: Design High-Performing Architectures (24% — 5 questions) ──

VALUES ('97437709-2fdc-4296-9396-c33bb88978fa', 'a1ad165a-7df4-48be-9efc-9f341ccb5117', 12, 'A CloudFront distribution with an S3 origin experiences a large volume of cache misses for a specific set of less-popular but still meaningfully-requested objects, each cache miss requiring CloudFront to fetch from the origin — potentially from many different edge locations independently, each treating the miss separately. What CloudFront feature reduces this redundant origin load?');
VALUES ('97437709-2fdc-4296-9396-c33bb88978fa', 'c6ff76fc-4dc7-4e22-b56c-be7895a567c0', 'Origin Shield adds an additional caching layer, positioned between CloudFront''s many geographically distributed edge locations and the origin — by routing cache-miss traffic through this consolidated layer first, multiple edge locations experiencing a miss for the SAME object within a similar window can be served from Origin Shield''s own cache rather than each edge location independently hitting the origin separately, directly reducing the total request volume/load reaching the origin (particularly valuable for origins with real capacity constraints, like an on-premises server behind Direct Connect, or simply to reduce S3 request costs at scale). Global Accelerator (covered in Paper 2) improves network ROUTING performance for TCP/UDP traffic over the AWS backbone — an entirely different mechanism from consolidating cache-miss requests. S3 Transfer Acceleration speeds up direct uploads TO S3 over long distances — also unrelated to this CloudFront-to-origin cache-miss consolidation scenario. This is a real, purpose-built CloudFront feature specifically for this class of origin-load problem.');

VALUES ('8c85a2de-3df1-4344-a14f-1f21c840c95a', 'a1ad165a-7df4-48be-9efc-9f341ccb5117', 13, 'A company is migrating a legacy on-premises application that communicates using the JMS (Java Message Service) protocol to AWS, and wants to preserve this existing protocol-specific messaging code with minimal rewriting, rather than re-architecting the application to use SQS/SNS''s different native APIs immediately. What AWS service is designed for this kind of protocol-compatible message broker migration?');
VALUES ('8c85a2de-3df1-4344-a14f-1f21c840c95a', '994391ca-6fa0-453e-9eed-a816ad2cdd9b', 'Amazon MQ is specifically positioned for this "migrate an existing protocol-dependent application with minimal rewrite" scenario — unlike SQS and SNS, which are AWS-native services with their own specific APIs (requiring application code changes to adopt), Amazon MQ supports industry-standard messaging protocols (JMS, AMQP 0-9-1, MQTT, STOMP, WebSocket) via managed ActiveMQ or RabbitMQ broker engines, letting an application that already speaks one of these standard protocols connect with little to no messaging-layer code change, easing a lift-and-shift-style migration before any longer-term modernization to SQS/SNS''s native APIs is undertaken (if ever). SQS does NOT natively speak JMS or other legacy protocols directly — it has its own distinct, AWS-specific API, which is exactly why Amazon MQ exists as a separate, protocol-compatible option for this specific migration need. Kinesis Data Streams (covered in Paper 4) is for high-volume real-time streaming ingestion, an entirely different use case and protocol model from a JMS-based message broker migration.');

VALUES ('d5517e04-dbf1-44bf-9144-ab2033d7f190', 'a1ad165a-7df4-48be-9efc-9f341ccb5117', 14, 'A startup''s Aurora PostgreSQL database experiences highly variable, unpredictable traffic — near zero for hours, then sudden bursts to a high load — making a fixed-capacity instance either wastefully over-provisioned most of the time or under-provisioned during bursts. What Aurora capability automatically and rapidly scales database capacity up and down to match this pattern?');
VALUES ('d5517e04-dbf1-44bf-9144-ab2033d7f190', '0cc97dff-1bfe-4164-98fa-0db616e5957d', 'Aurora Serverless (v2) is specifically designed for workloads with variable, unpredictable, or intermittent demand — instead of provisioning a fixed instance size sized for either the average load (under-provisioned during bursts) or the peak load (wastefully over-provisioned the rest of the time), it automatically adjusts capacity in fine-grained increments based on actual real-time load, scaling down to a low baseline during idle periods and scaling up rapidly as demand increases, directly matching the "near zero for hours, then sudden bursts" pattern described without requiring any manual instance-resize operation or advance capacity planning for the burst. RDS Multi-AZ (covered in Paper 1) provides HIGH AVAILABILITY via a synced standby — it has no automatic compute-capacity SCALING function of its own; a Multi-AZ instance is still a fixed-size instance (just with a failover partner), a genuinely different concern from elastic capacity scaling. S3 Intelligent-Tiering (covered in Paper 1) is an S3 storage-class feature, entirely unrelated to Aurora database compute capacity.');

VALUES ('5e3e85dd-8de7-4437-af4b-10919f914670', 'a1ad165a-7df4-48be-9efc-9f341ccb5117', 15, 'A company has grown from 3 VPCs to over 30 VPCs across multiple accounts, all needing to communicate with each other and with an on-premises data center. Managing dozens of individual VPC peering connections (each a separate point-to-point link) has become an operational burden. What AWS service simplifies this into a centralized hub-and-spoke model?');
VALUES ('5e3e85dd-8de7-4437-af4b-10919f914670', '4313a2f9-36f7-4db5-9ab6-35510035b93e', 'Transit Gateway solves exactly this operational-scale problem: rather than each VPC needing a SEPARATE point-to-point peering connection to every other VPC it needs to reach (which grows quadratically — dozens of VPCs means potentially hundreds of individual peering connections to create and maintain, each with its own route table entries), each VPC (and on-premises network, connected via Direct Connect or a Site-to-Site VPN) instead makes ONE connection to the central Transit Gateway, which then handles routing between all attached networks via centrally managed route tables — turning an unwieldy full-mesh topology into a simpler hub-and-spoke one. VPC Peering (a real, valid AWS feature for simpler, smaller-scale needs) is explicitly POINT-TO-POINT by design and does NOT automatically consolidate into a hub model on its own — peering connections are not even transitive (VPC A peered with B, and B peered with C, does NOT let A reach C through B without a direct A-C peering connection), which is precisely the scaling limitation Transit Gateway exists to solve. Route 53 (DNS) serves an entirely unrelated function from VPC network connectivity.');

-- ── Domain: Design Cost-Optimized Architectures (20% — 4 questions) ──

VALUES ('5789bc2b-a71a-4687-b3d8-41656c07a58a', 'a1ad165a-7df4-48be-9efc-9f341ccb5117', 16, 'A company runs a fault-tolerant, interruptible batch processing workload on Amazon ECS using Fargate, and wants to apply the same kind of steep discount Spot Instances provide for EC2 to this containerized, serverless-compute workload. What Fargate option provides this?');
VALUES ('5789bc2b-a71a-4687-b3d8-41656c07a58a', 'e89bece0-0ce2-40db-8b09-1664f9c2c852', 'Fargate Spot extends the same underlying spare-capacity-at-a-discount model that EC2 Spot Instances use (covered in Paper 1) to Fargate''s serverless container compute — eligible ECS tasks run on spare Fargate capacity at a meaningfully lower price than standard (On-Demand-equivalent) Fargate pricing, with the same fundamental trade-off: AWS can interrupt/reclaim that capacity, so Fargate Spot is appropriate specifically for the same category of fault-tolerant, checkpoint-able, interruption-tolerant batch/async workloads that make sense for EC2 Spot, not for tasks requiring guaranteed continuous uptime. This is a real, distinct Fargate pricing/capacity option (not simply Fargate''s always-on default pricing), and it is commonly combined with standard Fargate in the same ECS service (a mixed capacity provider strategy) to balance cost savings against guaranteed baseline availability. Direct Connect (dedicated network connectivity) has no relationship to Fargate compute pricing at all.');

VALUES ('88ad02f9-6cc3-4fef-aa4a-08c0105c10b3', 'a1ad165a-7df4-48be-9efc-9f341ccb5117', 17, 'A company has AWS Budgets configured with alert notifications when spending approaches a threshold, but wants an additional layer that automatically takes a real corrective ACTION (not just sending a notification for a human to act on) — for example, automatically applying a restrictive IAM policy to stop further resource creation once a hard spending limit is reached. What AWS Budgets capability provides this?');
VALUES ('88ad02f9-6cc3-4fef-aa4a-08c0105c10b3', 'fc394a63-57b7-4b90-b718-4eb56ea6bbff', 'AWS Budgets Actions extends basic Budgets (covered in Paper 3, which only sends alert notifications for a human to act on) into genuine automated remediation — an administrator can configure a specific action (attaching a restrictive IAM policy or SCP to limit further spending-driving actions, or targeting specific EC2/RDS resources to stop) that executes AUTOMATICALLY once a defined budget threshold is breached, either with or without requiring manual approval at the moment of triggering, depending on configuration. This directly addresses the "notification alone isn''t enough, I need an actual automatic guardrail" need described. Basic AWS Budgets alerts genuinely are notification-only WITHOUT Actions configured — Actions is a distinct, additional capability layered on top, not budgets'' only behavior. Inspector (vulnerability scanning) and Trusted Advisor (best-practice recommendations, covered in Paper 4) both serve entirely different functions with no budget-triggered automated remediation capability of their own.');

VALUES ('a42b6806-864f-4609-8ee1-3dbedc648e91', 'a1ad165a-7df4-48be-9efc-9f341ccb5117', 18, 'A company has data that is accessed infrequently, is not critical (it can be regenerated from another source if lost), and does not require the resilience of being stored across multiple Availability Zones. Which S3 storage class offers the lowest cost specifically by intentionally storing data in only ONE Availability Zone rather than the standard multi-AZ redundancy?');
VALUES ('a42b6806-864f-4609-8ee1-3dbedc648e91', '991e75fc-67d4-4865-9505-128d480941fc', 'S3 One Zone-IA is specifically the storage class that deliberately trades away multi-AZ resilience (most S3 storage classes, including Standard and Standard-IA, redundantly store data across a minimum of 3 Availability Zones) for a lower storage cost than Standard-IA — data in One Zone-IA exists in only ONE AZ, meaning a genuine, physical loss/destruction of that specific AZ would result in real data loss, which is exactly why AWS''s own guidance is to use it only for infrequently-accessed data that is either easily recreatable from another source, or a secondary copy of data that''s durably stored elsewhere already, or otherwise non-critical enough to accept this specific risk in exchange for the cost savings — precisely matching the scenario described. S3 Standard (covered in Paper 1) uses the standard multi-AZ redundancy model, not single-AZ. Glacier Deep Archive (an archival class) has retrieval times measured in HOURS, not milliseconds, and also uses multi-AZ storage — this option combines two separate, incorrect claims about Deep Archive.');

VALUES ('236b8eea-3449-466c-983e-92340390744c', 'a1ad165a-7df4-48be-9efc-9f341ccb5117', 19, 'A company suspects their RDS instance is over-provisioned relative to its actual database-specific workload (query performance, wait events, connection patterns) but Compute Optimizer''s recommendations (covered in Paper 2) focus more generally on CPU/memory/network utilization rather than deep database-specific performance analysis. What RDS-specific feature provides deeper database performance insight to inform right-sizing decisions?');
VALUES ('236b8eea-3449-466c-983e-92340390744c', 'ef28686d-764b-40d5-b20d-107a815a09b0', 'RDS Performance Insights is specifically designed to go beyond general infrastructure-level metrics (CPU, memory, network — the kind Compute Optimizer analyzes) into genuine DATABASE-level performance visibility: it shows overall database load, breaks that load down by the specific SQL queries contributing most to it, and surfaces wait events (what the database is actually spending time waiting ON — lock contention, I/O, CPU) — this level of detail is what lets an administrator distinguish "this instance is over-provisioned and can be downsized" from "this instance is correctly sized but a specific unoptimized query is the real problem" (a right-sizing decision general compute metrics alone cannot make reliably, since high CPU could stem from genuinely needing more capacity OR from one badly-written query that more capacity would only mask, not fix). RDS Multi-AZ (covered in Paper 1) provides HA/failover — no query-level performance analysis capability of its own. Direct Connect provides dedicated network connectivity, entirely unrelated to database query performance visibility.');

VALUES ('4c91c213-46b7-44a6-b756-81b8721749f9', 'a1ad165a-7df4-48be-9efc-9f341ccb5117', 20, 'A company wants to understand not just their CURRENT month-to-date AWS spending, but also get a data-driven FORECAST of what their spending is likely to be for the rest of the current month and future months, based on historical usage trends, to support more accurate budget planning. What Cost Explorer capability provides this?');
VALUES ('4c91c213-46b7-44a6-b756-81b8721749f9', 'cb7313fb-9766-40f7-99a2-201e01b6972e', 'Cost Explorer (covered in Paper 3 for its historical analysis capability) also includes a genuine forecasting feature — using historical cost and usage patterns, it projects likely future spending for upcoming periods, giving a data-driven basis for budget planning beyond simply looking backward at what has already been spent. This forward-looking view is a real, distinct Cost Explorer capability, not merely historical reporting — directly useful for setting realistic AWS Budgets thresholds (covered earlier in this paper and in Paper 3) informed by an actual trend-based projection rather than a guess. AWS Config (configuration compliance tracking, covered in Paper 3) and GuardDuty (behavioral threat detection, covered in Paper 4) both serve entirely unrelated functions with no cost-forecasting capability of their own — a clear category-mismatch distractor pattern.');
